# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-soundblaster-gain.rules
#
# The Sound Blaster Play! 3 (041e:324d) powers up with its onboard "Speaker"
# digital attenuator at -20dB (55%), not the 0dB it's capable of — this is the
# DAC's own default, not anything Linux or MPD sets.
#
# mpd.conf's Local output uses mixer_type "software": MPD's volume control
# scales PCM samples in software and never touches this ALSA hardware
# control, by design (it's what keeps a route-agnostic volume knob working
# the same way on Bluetooth/AirPlay too). That means nothing ever moves this
# control off its power-on default — every reboot and every USB
# unplug/replug quietly puts a fixed -20dB pad in front of an otherwise
# correctly-configured bit-perfect chain. Measured: 20dB is roughly a 10x
# amplitude cut, enough on its own to explain needing far more downstream
# gain (car head unit volume) than a normal source, which is what raises the
# noise floor and ground-loop hum along with it.
#
# Pin it to 0dB (max) every time the card appears. MPD's software volume
# still does all the real attenuation on top of this fixed ceiling.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="sound", KERNEL=="controlC*", \
    ATTRS{idVendor}=="041e", ATTRS{idProduct}=="324d", \
    RUN+="/opt/rpi-player/bin/soundblaster-gain"
